Subject: revoke what you free, not what you remember

A revoke record tells recovery "this block was freed; do not replay older
copies of it over whatever lives there now". lwext4 emitted one only for
blocks with a live block_rec -- blocks still tracked by an uncheckpointed
transaction of the current session.

But the log holds replayable records back to wherever the on-disk tail
points, which reaches further than the checkpoint queue: a block journaled
three transactions ago, checkpointed, freed, and reused as file data is
exactly the block a replay will clobber -- file data is never journaled, so
the replayed metadata wins -- and it had no block_rec, so it got no revoke.

Measured: a workload that creates forty one-block files and deletes them
emitted zero revoke blocks. Linux revokes on free unconditionally, for this
reason.

The asymmetry decides the design. A revoke for a block the log never mentions
costs four bytes and suppresses nothing; a missing revoke for a block the log
does mention rewrites history over live data. So: revoke every free. The one
case that needs care -- free then reallocate as metadata inside the same
transaction -- was already handled: jbd_trans_set_block_dirty cancels the
pending revoke when the block is re-journaled.

This is also a correctness precondition for letting the on-disk tail lag
(next patch): replaying already-checkpointed transactions is only idempotent
if every block freed since carries a revoke.

Upstream may prefer a metadata-only qualifier at the balloc call sites to
keep large-file deletes from writing a revoke entry per data block; the
unconditional form is correct either way.
